[THOUGHT] Absence of constitutional symptoms and oral lesions makes systemic infections (e.g., advanced syphilis, TB, HIV-related OIs) and autoimmune mucocutaneous disorders (e.g., Behçet’s, pemphigus) less likely — though early or latent infection cannot be ruled out without testing. The clinical picture remains focused on two persistent, painless, crusted lesions — one genital, one distal digit — suggesting either localized chronic infection (e.g., HPV-related verruca vulgaris or bowenoid papulosis), spirochetal infection (syphilis), or early squamous cell carcinoma (SCC).  

Next, I must gather domain #4: past medical and surgical history. Key items include:  
- History of STIs (especially syphilis, HPV, HIV), which would increase risk for atypical presentations;  
- Immunosuppression (e.g., HIV, transplant, chronic steroid use), which predisposes to persistent viral infections or SCC;  
- Skin cancer history (e.g., prior SCC, BCC, melanoma);  
- Chronic inflammatory conditions (e.g., lichen sclerosus — a risk factor for penile SCC).  

I will ask about prior STIs and immunosuppression first — highest-yield elements for this presentation.

[QUERY] Have you ever been diagnosed with syphilis, herpes, HPV, HIV, or any other sexually transmitted infection? And have you ever been told you have a weakened immune system — for example, due to HIV, chemotherapy, long-term steroid use, or an organ transplant?

